+/*
+ * umi Bluetooth: the Bluetooth side of the QCA6390 -- the Wi-Fi's chip -- on the UART of QUP
+ * serial engine 6 (HCI over H4). Nothing runs unless boot-arg sweetbt=N asks for steps 1..N; they
+ * run in a thread of their own, sweetbtdelay seconds (default 30: after the Wi-Fi thread has voted
+ * the chip's shared rails) after sweet_ufs_init() starts it.
+ *
+ * Adapted from project-BadApple (github.com/aTechGeekDev/ios-apollo, xnu 0136, 0137, 0141, 0143,
+ * 0145: bsd/dev/sweet_bt.c; MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Leo Malik, dipa-studio), itself ported from
+ * the Xiaomi Mi 10 port of github.com/JopangJovan/hackios-xnu-kernel-umi, devices/xiaomi-mi-10
+ * (umi_bluetooth.c, hakios.bluetooth.h, hakios.bluetooth.cpp, umi_rpmh.c; MIT). umi's DT wires
+ * the chip as apollo's: BT_EN GPIO 21 (cnss-qca6390 qcom,bt-en-gpio), SW_CTRL GPIO 124
+ * (qcom,sw-ctrl-gpio), qupv3_se6_4uart at 0x998000 on GPIO 16-19 (CTS, RTS, TX, RX).
+ *
+ * The firmware is the phone's own, read (read-only, like the Wi-Fi's from "modem") from the FAT
+ * of the "bluetooth" partition: image/htbtfw20.tlv (patch) and image/htnv20.bin (NVM); boot-args
+ * sweetbtpart, sweetbtfw and sweetbtnv name others. A missing file lists the directory.
+ *
+ *   1  read   SE6's GCC clocks and the pins
+ *   2  write  SE6's clocks voted for a moment: the UART's GENI registers; put back
+ *   3  write  ... and the UART looped back on itself inside the SoC (nothing reaches the chip)
+ *   4  write  rails, BT_EN, the UART at 115200 baud (H4): HCI reset and the version from the
+ *             chip's ROM; then BT_EN low again and the UART put back
+ *   5  write  ... the UART moved to sweetbtbaud (below), the firmware (patch, then NVM), reset, the
+ *             version it runs, the address (/chosen mac-address-bluetooth0), and what the radio
+ *             hears: 3 s of LE scan and 4 s of classic inquiry; then BT_EN low again
+ *   6  write  as 5 without the scans, and the radio stays up for iOS: an H4 endpoint (the kernel
+ *             control "com.sweet.bluetooth", for bluetoothd) and the registry entry "bluetooth"
+ *             that bluetoothd looks for (iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetBluetooth.cpp)
+ *
+ * The chip's ROM talks at 115200 baud. From step 5 on the UART is moved to boot-arg sweetbtbaud
+ * (default 921600; 115200 keeps it there) before the firmware goes in, as Linux's hci_qca does, and
+ * the NVM is made to say the same speed. Should the new speed not work the chip is started over at
+ * 115200. No interrupt is taken: the FIFOs are polled. SW_CTRL is an input that is read, as
+ * Android's btpower.c has it (sweetbtswctrl=1 drives it high instead). The ADT's address is given
+ * to the chip (0xfc14, as Linux's qca_set_bdaddr), else it keeps the NVM's.
+ *
+ * project-BadApple's licence, for this file, sweet_bt.h and SweetBluetooth.cpp:
+ *   Copyright (c) 2026 Leo Malik
+ *   Copyright (c) 2026 dipa-studio
+ *   Permission is hereby granted, free of charge, to any person obtaining a copy of this software
+ *   and associated documentation files (the "Software"), to deal in the Software without
+ *   restriction, including without limitation the rights to use, copy, modify, merge, publish,
+ *   distribute, sublicense, and/or sell copies of the Software, and to permit persons to whom the
+ *   Software is furnished to do so, subject to the following conditions: The above copyright
+ *   notice and this permission notice shall be included in all copies or substantial portions of
+ *   the Software. THE SOFTWARE IS PROVIDED "AS IS", WITHOUT WARRANTY OF ANY KIND, EXPRESS OR
+ *   IMPLIED, INCLUDING BUT NOT LIMITED TO THE WARRANTIES OF MERCHANTABILITY, FITNESS FOR A
+ *   PARTICULAR PURPOSE AND NONINFRINGEMENT. IN NO EVENT SHALL THE AUTHORS OR COPYRIGHT HOLDERS BE
+ *   LIABLE FOR ANY CLAIM, DAMAGES OR OTHER LIABILITY, WHETHER IN AN ACTION OF CONTRACT, TORT OR
+ *   OTHERWISE, ARISING FROM, OUT OF OR IN CONNECTION WITH THE SOFTWARE OR THE USE OR OTHER DEALINGS
+ *   IN THE SOFTWARE.
+ */
